---
import Project from '../../layouts/Project.astro'
import { projectGroups } from '../../data/cv'
import { authors, u } from '../../lib'
// the notes that carry the background for this page
const READING = [
  { id: 'sasp', t: 'Senescence and SASP: the cells that stop dividing but keep signalling', d: 'what the secretory phenotype is, why there is no single marker, and the gene sets used to score it' },
  { id: 'de-tools', t: 'Differential expression tools: DESeq2, PyDESeq2, MAST', d: 'the tests behind the comparison above' },
]
const lipid = projectGroups.find(x => x.heading === 'Manuscripts')!.items.find(p => p.title.startsWith('Lipid'))!
---
<Project slug="lipid"
  lede="Senescent cells accumulate lipid, and they signal. The question is whether the first has anything to do with the second."
  facts={[
    { k: 'status', v: 'manuscript in preparation' },
    { k: 'collaboration', v: 'Mayo Clinic' },
    { k: 'corresponding', v: 'Dr. Diana Jurk' },
    { k: 'alongside', v: `<a href="${u('projects/masld/')}">Aging-related MASLD</a>` },
  ]}>
  <figure slot="plate" class="plate">
    <img src={u('assets/fig/lipid-volcano-lipid.png')} alt="A volcano plot of lipid-high against lipid-low senescent cells, with the lipid-related differentially expressed genes marked in green — C3, ZC3H12A, IL1B, C1QTNF1, TNFAIP3, NFKBIA, PTGS1, EDNRB, DHCR24. Almost everything significant moves up in the lipid-high cells." width="1288" height="915" loading="eager">
    <figcaption><span>Lipid-high against lipid-low senescent cells</span><span>in vitro bulk RNA-seq · lipid-related genes in green</span></figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>The project</h2>
  <p>This is a collaboration with Mayo Clinic on how much lipid a senescent cell carries and how loudly it signals — the senescence-associated secretory phenotype, or SASP. Senescent cells differ from one another in lipid content, so the lipid-high and lipid-low populations can be separated and sequenced. Almost everything that differs between them shifts in one direction, and the genes that shift furthest are the secreted ones.</p>
  <figure class="plate inline">
    <img class="tall" src={u('assets/fig/lipid-heatmap.png')} alt="A clustered heatmap of inflammatory and lipid-metabolic genes in lipid-high against lipid-low senescent cells. The two groups separate, with cytokine genes high in the lipid-high cells and lipid-handling genes high in the lipid-low ones." width="774" height="986" loading="lazy">
    <figcaption><span>The same comparison, clustered</span><span>row z-score · the two groups separate on inflammatory and lipid genes</span></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>Clustered on those genes, the two populations come apart: the cytokines sit with the lipid-high cells and the lipid-handling genes with the lipid-low ones.</p>
  <p><span class="role">My role</span> the transcriptomic analysis across the public, <i>in vitro</i> and <i>in vivo</i> senescence data.</p>
  <p class="note">The manuscript is in preparation. The figures above are examples of the analysis, not figures from it.</p>

  <h2>The paper</h2>
  <div class="paper cell"><p class="t">{lipid.title}</p><p class="a" set:html={authors(lipid.authors)} /><p class="v">{lipid.status} · {lipid.org}</p></div>
  <p class="note">The lipid processes this work approaches from the cell side also come out of public liver data — <a href={u('projects/masld/')}>Aging-related MASLD</a>.</p>

  <h2>Background, in the notes</h2>
  <ul class="reading">
    {READING.map(r => <li><a href={u(`blog/${r.id}-en/`)}><span class="t">{r.t}</span><span class="d">{r.d}</span></a></li>)}
  </ul>
</Project>
<!-- a portrait figure, so it is centred at a readable width rather than stretched -->
<style>.plate img.tall { width: auto; max-width: min(100%, 440px); margin: 18px auto 6px; }</style>
